audit+='''<h2>Showcase inventory and remaining work</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Surface</th><th>What is prepared</th><th>Before showing it as a current polished example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>PySide material / VFX</td><td>Updated action emphasis, style sheet and nine new captures; existing tests passed.</td><td>Material-start traversal was checked with actual offscreen Tab key events. Still verify all states, target display scaling and live engine selection/stale-state/recovery behavior. Replace stale deck crops and code excerpts when integrating.</td></tr><tr><td>Native Unreal Slate panel</td><td>Retained as a distinct native implementation; no claim that a parented Qt window is a docked native panel.</td><td>Check equivalent labels, scope, preview, guarded Apply and recovery in the actual panel. Capture current states and record any intentional host-specific differences.</td></tr><tr><td>Blender / Maya and other course demos</td><td>Included in the audit scope; existing teaching roles retained.</td><td>Inspect each UI that will actually appear, use a prepared task, check reset, labels, focus, errors and projection readability. Do not infer parity from PySide tests.</td></tr><tr><td>GOLF+ overlay / Spline Suite</td><td>Historical source images, feedback and playbook identified.</td><td>Capture current matching task and failure/recovery states. Compare implementation against the playbook; no GOLF+ production code was changed by this pass.</td></tr><tr><td>Lecture screenshots and examples</td><td>Replacement evidence and exact current slide anchors are staged.</td><td>Replace obsolete screenshots during the deck integration; label practice data and conceptual telemetry. Render and inspect every changed slide.</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Implementation bridge</h2><p>Use one short worked example: the material task, a low-fidelity flow, the real Qt layout and the state-driven Preview/Apply behavior. Let students trace one design rule into code. Keep the full implementation and native Slate comparison in the companion material. A second in-engine UI build would compete with the design lesson.</p><p>Figma is useful for testing flow before backend work. An Editor Utility Widget provides a familiar in-engine option, but it is a different implementation path from this project’s native C++ Slate panel. Choose the fidelity that answers the current question.</p><h2>Verification record</h2><p><code>Scripts/dev.py test</code>: 87 passed. <code>Scripts/assistants.py --render</code>: nine refreshed states, generated 15 October 2026. Host restrictions were respected: no live Unreal launch. Contrast measurements and browser checks are recorded in <a href="verification.json">verification.json</a> after review validation.</p>'''
audit=audit.replace('<h2>Concrete changes</h2>','<div class="notice"><strong>Revised delivery:</strong> use prepared state comparisons and readable code as the baseline explanation. A successful live run is not required to cover the concept. Preserve colleague demonstration time, and manually validate any instructor operation chosen for live execution.</div><h2>Concrete changes</h2>')
audit=audit.replace('<tr><td>GOLF+ overlay / Spline Suite</td><td>Historical source images, feedback and playbook identified.</td><td>Capture current matching task and failure/recovery states. Compare implementation against the playbook; no GOLF+ production code was changed by this pass.</td></tr>','<tr><td>GOLF+ Course Generator / historical suite examples</td><td>The supplied six-stage Course Generator screenshot is the main visual example; older overlay and Spline Suite images remain process history.</td><td>No new capture is required to teach its visible hierarchy. Capture warning, progress and outcome only for claims about behavior or a matched before/after. Reconcile playbook terminology; no GOLF+ production code was changed.</td></tr>')
